nsmitter type 8732 (measuring transducer with electronic calculator), Flow Sensor types 8705 and 8711. So components[]:\n  - role: \"measuring transducer / transmitter (electronic calculator)\", type_designations: [\"8732\"]\n  - role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"8705\", \"8711\"], alternatives? The description: \"consist of flow sensor types 8705 and 8711 and of measuring transducer with the electronic calculator type 8732\". Hmm — it's a system combining sensor + transmitter. The sensor types 8705 and 8711 are alternatives (8705 standard flanges, 8711 wafer). Actually page 3-4 tables describe type 8711 (wafer connection). Page 3 heading says \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter types 8732 and Magnetic Flow Sensor types 8711 with the lining material made by PTFE\". Connection type: Wafer. So the tables are for 8711 sensors. 8705 has standard flanges.\n\n  I'll record components:\n  - components[0]: role \"transmitter / measuring transducer with electronic calculator\", type_designations [\"8732\"]\n  - components[1]: role \"flow sensor\", type_designations [\"8705\", \"8711\"], alternatives \"OR\", characteristics: connection types differ.\n\n**model_family:**\nThe tables give characteristics per DN (nominal diameter): DN 4, 8, 15, 25, 40 (page 3) and DN 50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200 (page 4). This is a model matrix keyed on nominal diameter. So model_family models = DN4, DN8, DN15, DN25, DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN150, DN200.\n\nWait — page 4 table also has \"Accuracy class: 2\" for DN50 and \"1\" for the rest (colspan 5 for 65-200). Let me check: row `<td>Accuracy class:</td><td>2</td><td colspan=\"5\">1</td></tr>` — so DN50 class 2, DN65–200 class 1.\n\n**matrix_tables:**\nTwo tables: page 3 (DN 4–40) and page 4 (DN 50–200). I should emit them as matrix_tables with rows per DN.\n\nLet me extract page 3 table (sensor 8711, PTFE liner):\n\nColumns: nominal_diameter_mm, overload_flowrate_q4_m3h, permanent_flowrate_q3_m3h, transitional_flowrate_q2_m3h, minimum_flowrate_q1_m3h, ratio_q3_q1, ratio_q2_q1, ratio_q4_q3, accuracy_class, mpe_lower_zone, mpe_upper_zone, temperature_class, max_admissible_pressure_bar, max_pressure_loss_bar, environmental_class, electromagnetic_environment, indicating_range_m3, resolution_l, min_straight_inlet, min_straight_outlet, orientation, length_mm, connection_type, power supply...\n\nRow values for DN4: Q4 ≤0.50, Q3 ≤0.40, Q2 ≥0.00512, Q1 ≥0.00320\nDN8: ≤2.0, ≤1.6, ≥0.0205, ≥0.0128\nDN15: ≤7.9, ≤6.3, ≥0.0504, ≥0.0320\nDN25: ≤20.0, ≤16.0, ≥0.128, ≥0.080\nDN40: ≤50.0, ≤40.0, ≥0.320, ≥0.200\n\nRatio Q3/Q1: ≤125² for DN4,8; ≤200² for DN15,25,40 (colspan 2 and 3).\nRatio Q2/Q1: 1.6; Q4/Q3: 1.25; Accuracy class 2; MPE_l ±5%; MPE_u: ±2% ≤30°C, ±3% >30°C; T90 PTFE; max admissible pressure 0 to 19 bar for DN4,8,15 (colspan 3) and 0 to 50 bar for DN25,40 (colspan 2); max pressure loss 0.16 bar; env B and C; E1 and E2; indicating range 9999 to 99999 m³ (note \"9 999 to 99 999\"); resolution: 0.01 (colspan 2 → DN4,8), 0.1 (colspan 2 → DN15,25), 1 (DN40); min straight length inlet 5, outlet 3; arbitrary orientation; length: 55,55,56,57,73 mm; wafer; power AC: Umax 250, Umin 90, 50 or 60 Hz; DC: Umax 42, Umin 12.\n\nPage 4 (DN 50–200):\nDN50: Q4 ≤78.8, Q3 ≤63.0, Q2 ≥0.504, Q1 ≥0.315\nDN65: ≤125, ≤100, ≥1.60, ≥1.00\nDN80: ≤200, ≤160, ≥2.56, ≥1.60\nDN100: ≤313, ≤250, ≥4.0, ≥2.5\nDN150: ≤788, ≤630, ≥10.1, ≥6.3\nDN200: ≤1250, ≤1000, ≥16.0, ≥10.0\n\nRatio Q3/Q1 ≤100²; Q2/Q1 1.6; Q4/Q3 1.25; accuracy class: 2 (DN50), 1 (DN65–200); MPE_l: ±5% (DN50), ±3% (DN65–200); MPE_u: ≤30°C: ±2% (DN50), ±1% (rest); >30°C: ±3% (DN50), ±2% (rest); T90 PTFE; env B and C; E1 E2; max admissible pressure 0 to 50 bar; pressure loss 0.16 bar; indicating range 999999 to 9999999 m³; resolution: 1 (colspan 5 → DN50–150), 10 (DN200); lengths: 84 (DN50), 120 (colspan 2 → DN65,80), 149 (DN100), 180 (DN150), 230 (DN200); wafer; power same.\n\n**characteristics.model_level:**\nI should populate model_level entries for attributes that vary per model (DN): q4, q3, q2, q1, max_admissible_pressure, resolution, length, accuracy_class (varies in page 4), mpe (varies in page 4). But this could get huge. The schema wants model_level matrix for varying attributes. Given two matrix_tables preserve full detail, I can include key model_level entries: overload_flowrate_q4, permanent_flowrate_q3, transitional_flowrate_q2, minimum_flowrate_q1, nominal_diameter, length_of_water_meter, resolution_of_indicating_device, max_admissible_pressure, accuracy_class.\n\nThe Q values have ≤/≥ bounds — value as {min: null, max: X} for Q3, Q4 (upper bounds), {min: X, max: null} for Q1, Q2 (lower bounds).\n\nAlso ratio Q3/Q1 varies by group: ≤125 (DN4,8), ≤200 (DN15–40), ≤100 (DN50–200) with footnote 2. This could be config_level or model_level. I'll make it model_level with groups.\n\nFootnotes:\n1) The value of Q3 shall be chosen from the R5 line of ISO 3:1973.\n2) The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen from the R10 line from ISO 3:1973 and this value shall be higher than 10.\n\nThese appear on both pages 3 and 4 (same text). Emit once each.\n\n**type_level characteristics:**\n- temperature_class: \"T90 for PTFE liner\" — value string \"T90\", maybe note PTFE liner. I'll put value \"T90\" with note? Schema is {value, unit_symbol...}. I'll do value: \"T90\" and maybe a separate \"temperature_class_note\". Simpler: value \"T90 (PTFE liner)\"? Rule says no trailing punctuation; strings allowed. I'll use value: \"T90\" with a separate type_level characteristic \"liner_temperature_class_material\"? Hmm, too fussy. Just value \"T90 for PTFE liner\"? That's a string value; fine. Actually cleaner: \"temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T90\"}. And the PTFE lining is already in description. I'll keep \"T90\".\n\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6\n- ratio_q4_q3: 1.25\n- max_pressure_loss: 0.16 bar\n- environmental_class: \"B and C\" → value string \"B, C\"? I'll use \"B and C\" as string. Maybe {\"value\": [\"B\",\"C\"]}? Schema allows string. Use string \