73,84,120,120,149,180,230.\n11. indicating_range (m³): {9999–99999} for DN4–40; {999999–9999999} for DN50–200.\n12. mpe_upper_zone at temp ≤30°C: ±2% for DN4–50; ±1% DN65–200. mpe at >30°C: ±3% DN4–50; ±2% DN65–200. mpe_lower_zone: ±5% DN4–50; ±3% DN65–200.\n\nThat's a lot but thorough. Maybe I'll include mpe entries too. Let me include them as model_level with values as percent.\n\nActually, to keep the JSON manageable, I'll include these model_level attributes: nominal_diameter, q4, q3, q2, q1, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class, max_admissible_pressure, resolution_of_indicating_device, length_of_water_meter, indicating_range, mpe_lower_zone, mpe_upper_zone_at_30c_or_below... hmm naming. Let me name:\n- mpe_lower_flowrate_zone (unit %): values ±5 etc. Value numeric 5, unit \"%\" — sign? Value -5? MPE ±5% → value 5 with... I'd store value 5 and unit \"%\", the attribute name says MPE. Maybe value: \"±5%\"? Rules prefer numbers. I'll store value 5, unit \"%\", and attribute name maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone. Sign ambiguity acceptable; or use -5? I'll use 5.\n\nNames:\n- maximum_permitted_error_lower_flowrate_zone\n- maximum_permitted_error_upper_flowrate_zone_up_to_30c — awkward. Alternative: config_level with axis \"water_temperature\": conditions \"≤30 °C\" and \">30 °C\". That fits config_level! attribute maximum_permitted_error_upper_flowrate_zone, axis water_temperature, values [{condition:\"≤ 30 °C\", value 2, unit \"%\", model DN4-50...}. Hmm but condition needs to combine model + temp. config_level conditions are single dimension.\n\nSimpler: model_level entries:\n- mpe_upper_zone_water_le_30c: 2 (DN4–50), 1 (DN65–200)\n- mpe_upper_zone_water_gt_30c: 3 (DN4–50), 2 (DN65–200)\nLabel naming with le/gt is a bit awkward but clear. Alternatively omit MPEs from model_level since matrix_tables capture them. To limit size, I'll include them anyway — completeness is good.\n\nHmm, but the response size... It's fine; JSON can be long.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the